Yes. Presence can induce deference, attention, or submission, but size is only one way to generate it.

A person can have presence through physicality, but also through:

* competence — everyone knows they can execute;
* calm — they do not get pulled into other people’s tempo;
* certainty — not arrogance, but low internal wobble;
* status — others already expect their actions to matter;
* information — they know something others need;
* attention — they see more of the room than the room sees of them;
* moral authority — people trust their judgment;
* scarcity — their approval, time, or participation is difficult to obtain;
* coherence — words, posture, decisions, and values all point the same direction.

The common variable is something like perceived capacity + self-possession.

That is why “master your own blade” is the right second half. Whatever your blade is—strength, intelligence, charisma, anger, sexuality, humor, status, language—it becomes dangerous when it controls you more than you control it.

The strongest presence often comes from unused capacity. You can feel that someone could push harder, speak louder, dominate the interaction, or escalate—but they do not need to.

That creates a different signal:

\text{capacity} + \text{restraint} \rightarrow \text{credible presence}

Whereas uncontrolled display can signal the opposite:

\text{capacity} - \text{self-command} \rightarrow \text{instability}

And presence itself is relational. The same person can dominate one room and disappear in another because different environments reward different currencies: size in one, expertise in another, humor somewhere else, wealth, beauty, seniority, courage, fluency, or simply the ability to remain composed under pressure.

So the deeper skill is not “be intimidating.”

It is:

know what power you carry, know what environment you are in, and remain the one holding the handle.

That is what makes the blade an instrument rather than an impulse.